 ],
 "literal": "Listen, may you live, friend! To mother, / the high-status world also is small indeed. His mountain's / evening-rained, fragrance-emitting freshet-with, / morning-came, Malabar glory lily's entire root, / so that its soft leaves droop, embracing, and / bringing to the house and planting, indeed she did not forbid.",
 "translation": "Listen, my friend, may you live long! Even the high heavenly world is too small for mother's heart. For when I embraced the entire root of the Malabar glory lily—which came in the morning with the fragrant freshet of the evening rains on his mountain—so closely that its soft leaves drooped, and then brought it home and planted it, she did not forbid me at all.",
 "modern": "தோழியே, கேள்! நீ வாழ்க! அன்னைக்கு மேலுலகமும் மிகச் சிறியதே ஆகும். தலைவனுடைய மலையில் மாலையில் பெய்த மழையினால் உண்டான நறுமணம் வீசும் வெள்ளத்தோடு அடித்துக்கொண்டு வந்து, காலையில் கரை சேர்ந்த செங்காந்தள் செடியின் முழு வேரையும், அதன் மென்மையான இலைகள் குழைந்துபோகும்படி நான் தழுவிக்கொண்டதையும், அதை வீட்டிற்கு எடுத்துவந்து நட்டதையும் அன்னை தடுக்கவே இல்லை.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her friend in a kurinji (mountainous) landscape, expressing her joy at her mother's tacit approval of her love. The hero's mountain is described as having evening rains that wash down a beautiful glory lily in the morning freshet. The heroine embraced this lily so passionately that its soft leaves drooped, and then she brought it home to plant in their garden. Her mother, witnessing this, did not object or punish her, which the heroine interprets as a sign that her mother understands and accepts her love for the hero of that mountain. The key words are 'அன்னைக்கு' (to mother), 'முயங்கலும்' (embracing), and 'கடியாதோளே' (she did not forbid).",
